class Solution:
    def shadowPairs(self, nums: list[int]) -> int:
        """
        Count pairs (i, j) with i < j such that nums[i] < nums[j] and there
        is no index k (i < k < j) with nums[k] < nums[i] < nums[j].
        
        Approach (monotonic decreasing stack):
        For each j from left to right we want to count how many i < j can
        form a shadow pair ending at j.  The condition
            no k in (i, j) with nums[k] < nums[i] < nums[j]
        means that between i and j every element must be >= nums[i].
        If we walk j leftwards from the current position we stop as soon as
        we see a value strictly smaller than nums[i] (that would be an invalid
        k).  Therefore, for a fixed j the valid i's are exactly those indices
        that are "visible" from j when looking leftwards and that have a value
        smaller than nums[j].

        We maintain a stack of indices with strictly decreasing nums values.
        When we process nums[j] we pop all indices i from the stack whose
        value is <= nums[j] (they cannot serve as i for any future j because
        the popped value would be a smaller element between i and the new j).
        After popping, the stack top (if any) is the nearest index to the left
        with a value > nums[j].  All indices that were popped have value < nums[j]
        and are still "visible" from j because there is no smaller element between
        them and j (the popped elements themselves are >= the remaining ones).
        Each popped index forms a valid shadow pair (i, j).

        Moreover, after the pops the new element nums[j] may also pair with
        the current stack top (which has value > nums[j]), but that pair does
        not satisfy nums[i] < nums[j], so we ignore it.

        The total number of shadow pairs is simply the total number of pops
        performed throughout the scan.
        """
        stack: list[int] = []          # stores indices with decreasing nums values
        ans = 0
        for j, val in enumerate(nums):
            # pop all previous indices i with nums[i] <= val
            # those i are valid shadow pairs (i, j) because there is no
            # element smaller than nums[i] between i and j.
            while stack and nums[stack[-1]] <= val:
                stack.pop()
                ans += 1
            # push current index; it may serve as i for future j's
            stack.append(j)
        return ans
